tls (OpenSSL): the roots named in SSL_CERT_FILE, read once for every connection

On Android FFmpeg's connections are judged against the roots the app
writes to a file (Android's own: the system's, less the ones the user
turned off, and the ones the user added) and names in SSL_CERT_FILE.
Two things stood in the way of that.

OpenSSL reads SSL_CERT_FILE only where the kernel did not mark the
process as started with other rights than its parent's (AT_SECURE).
An Android app's process is so marked (seen on Android 15), so OpenSSL
read no file, had no roots, and turned down every certificate: a public
https stream failed with "unable to get local issuer certificate".

And OpenSSL read that file whole for every connection, ahead of its
handshake (SSL_CTX_set_default_verify_paths). With Android's roots, 145
certificates in 218 KB, that is about 3 ms of every connection on an
emulator on an Apple M-series Mac, and more on the slower processors of
phones and television boxes; an HLS stream whose server does not keep
its connections open makes one for every part.

So a connection that names no file of its own (`ca_file`) now takes the
roots of the file named in SSL_CERT_FILE, read with the plain getenv, from
one store read once and shared. The file's inode, size and time are
looked at for every connection, and the store is read again when they
change, so roots the app writes anew reach the next connection; one that
already took the old store keeps it. When no file is named, or it cannot
be read, a connection reads OpenSSL's defaults as before.

